#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100777 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a16c2c4f-38c3-5e8c-8962-3ad6c956748a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7321" :question/problem "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=120.$ Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100779 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "29ee4686-7e38-5000-9d89-58e572f84cd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7404" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074ac593-7a3a-5f93-b735-2ee808d813ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250612} {:answer/id #uuid "26237e43-f926-52c0-b38b-101da366fa24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=200\\,000P(0.000\\,8-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250882} {:answer/id #uuid "541bd8b2-71a6-5943-ad57-14b07db292c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=200\\,000P(0.000\\,8+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251242} {:answer/id #uuid "9e46f5d2-90e5-5cc9-8cba-3b3148314243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251919} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fd8307-82a8-58ee-8ea7-20ad8279337e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9fd8307-82a8-58ee-8ea7-20ad8279337e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252343} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f23058-cab9-507d-bf83-323581aa6b9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7231a27e-cc7a-5b74-bbc6-64de8a2ccd35" :question/math-academy-id "q-119286" :question/problem "The growth rate of the population $P(t)$ in a certain city is jointly proportional to the population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the city's carrying capacity and the population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,8$ and the maximum possible population is $200\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the population, the population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,8,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=200\\,000.$ Therefore, the population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c6621c-2a84-5be7-92b6-7a8d9a34cef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348816} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad50b10-a787-5de2-92ed-747b1d730ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348826} {:answer/id #uuid "465dbfcf-7f46-59e0-9e87-913b6084abf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349087} {:answer/id #uuid "938cb335-4e1f-58de-bf60-7c34ccdd9aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349765} {:answer/id #uuid "e8389761-f855-5a51-83d5-ab0558106e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c6621c-2a84-5be7-92b6-7a8d9a34cef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348816} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e90022-ffea-5ad7-b103-9f5780f7dcf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91925a6d-d444-5dc5-b5e4-01affa0e79dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83848" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=1,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ecad4cf-6f4e-5dbe-b398-a7011470a8ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.000\\,002P(50\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348575} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3c528d-02f7-5617-bad3-395c086edfd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000P(0.000\\,002+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349001} {:answer/id #uuid "53f761e0-2657-544a-ae8e-012f164d09f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349213} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8314de-e026-5b53-84ac-59d41ea31d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000P(0.000\\,002-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349306} {:answer/id #uuid "821c67f9-d1de-5e15-a4af-e7f59909a2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53f761e0-2657-544a-ae8e-012f164d09f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349213} :answer-field/id #uuid "e052f5ca-7248-5e20-b2b0-06564e0b1fa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07dd1e2f-c2b7-5df5-89ff-72a9c31796dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-83857" :question/problem "The growth rate of the seal population $P(t)$ on a certain South African island is jointly proportional to the seal population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the island's carrying capacity and the seal population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,002$ and the maximum possible seal population is $50\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the seal population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the seal population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the seal population, the seal population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,002,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000.$ Therefore, the seal population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000-P).\n$$"}]}
